1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,911
Here's something that sounds like a mistake.

2
00:00:02,911 --> 00:00:07,472
Point a telescope at a galaxy, then at an identical one much farther away.

3
00:00:07,472 --> 00:00:09,444
The far one should look smaller.

4
00:00:09,444 --> 00:00:11,108
And it does, up to a point.

5
00:00:11,108 --> 00:00:15,176
Then, if you keep going, the farther galaxy starts to look bigger.

6
00:00:15,875 --> 00:00:17,586
That's not an optical trick.

7
00:00:17,586 --> 00:00:23,023
It's a clue about why nobody, with any telescope, will ever see the edge of the universe.

8
00:00:23,023 --> 00:00:24,978
There are five walls in the way.

9
00:00:24,978 --> 00:00:26,628
Let's hit them, one by one.

10
00:00:27,327 --> 00:00:28,853
Wall number one is time.

11
00:00:28,853 --> 00:00:31,332
Light is fast, but not infinitely fast.

12
00:00:31,332 --> 00:00:33,938
So when you look far, you also look back.

13
00:00:33,938 --> 00:00:36,290
The Sun you see is eight minutes old.

14
00:00:36,290 --> 00:00:39,786
The Andromeda galaxy, two and a half million years old.

15
00:00:40,486 --> 00:00:41,698
Now go much deeper.

16
00:00:41,698 --> 00:00:49,990
At a redshift of one, a number astronomers use for distance in time, you're seeing light that left almost eight billion years ago.

17
00:00:49,990 --> 00:00:51,584
Before the Earth existed.

18
00:00:51,584 --> 00:00:53,052
Before the Sun existed.

19
00:00:53,752 --> 00:00:58,026
And the universe itself is only thirteen point eight billion years old.

20
00:00:58,026 --> 00:00:59,471
So there's a hard limit.

21
00:00:59,471 --> 00:01:02,782
You can't receive light that's older than the universe.

22
00:01:02,782 --> 00:01:07,056
Whatever is out there beyond that, its light simply hasn't arrived yet.

23
00:01:07,056 --> 00:01:07,538
Not dim.

24
00:01:07,538 --> 00:01:08,200
Not hidden.

25
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:09,525
Just still on its way.

26
00:01:09,525 --> 00:01:15,064
Every year that passes, a little more of it gets here, and our view grows a tiny bit deeper.

27
00:01:15,763 --> 00:01:17,671
Wall number two is the stretch.

28
00:01:17,671 --> 00:01:21,487
While that ancient light travels, space expands underneath it.

29
00:01:21,487 --> 00:01:23,333
And the light expands with it.

30
00:01:23,333 --> 00:01:25,363
Its waves get longer, and redder.

31
00:01:26,063 --> 00:01:29,845
The farthest galaxy ever confirmed is called MoM z fourteen.

32
00:01:29,845 --> 00:01:34,886
Its light left when the universe was about two hundred eighty million years old.

33
00:01:34,886 --> 00:01:40,432
Ultraviolet light from its young, hot stars arrives here more than fifteen times longer.

34
00:01:40,432 --> 00:01:43,331
Pushed right past red, deep into the infrared.

35
00:01:43,331 --> 00:01:46,607
Invisible to your eyes, and to Hubble's best camera.

36
00:01:47,307 --> 00:01:49,999
And that stretch is also a measuring tape.

37
00:01:49,999 --> 00:01:57,304
A redshift of fourteen point four four means every wave arrives fifteen point four four times longer than it left.

38
00:01:57,304 --> 00:02:04,737
Which means the universe itself has grown fifteen point four four times in every direction since that light set off.

39
00:02:05,437 --> 00:02:15,657
That's exactly why the James Webb telescope was built for infrared, with a sunshield the size of a tennis court, keeping it colder than minus two hundred twenty degrees.

40
00:02:15,657 --> 00:02:19,709
A warm telescope would glow in the same light it's trying to catch.

41
00:02:20,409 --> 00:02:22,777
Wall number three is a double penalty.

42
00:02:22,777 --> 00:02:24,958
Far away things are dim, of course.

43
00:02:24,958 --> 00:02:30,691
But in an expanding universe, they get dimmer much faster than distance alone would explain.

44
00:02:31,391 --> 00:02:34,074
Each photon loses energy as it's stretched.

45
00:02:34,074 --> 00:02:37,630
And the photons also arrive spread out, fewer per second.

46
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:45,178
Put those effects together, and the surface brightness of a galaxy falls with one plus the redshift, to the fourth power.

47
00:02:45,178 --> 00:02:47,985
At redshift one, that's sixteen times dimmer.

48
00:02:47,985 --> 00:02:54,410
For MoM z fourteen, about fifty seven thousand times dimmer, on top of the normal fading with distance.

49
00:02:55,110 --> 00:02:56,988
And here's the strangest part.

50
00:02:56,988 --> 00:02:59,931
The distant universe is playing in slow motion.

51
00:02:59,931 --> 00:03:05,253
An exploding star called a type one A supernova brightens and fades over a few weeks.

52
00:03:05,253 --> 00:03:10,199
In twenty twenty four, the Dark Energy Survey compared fifteen hundred of them.

53
00:03:10,199 --> 00:03:12,953
The ones at redshift one took twice as long.

54
00:03:12,953 --> 00:03:16,209
Exactly the stretch the expanding universe predicts.

55
00:03:16,909 --> 00:03:19,931
So the far universe is redder, dimmer, and slower.

56
00:03:19,931 --> 00:03:23,437
Every step outward, it gets harder to see anything at all.

57
00:03:24,137 --> 00:03:26,363
Wall number four is the funhouse mirror.

58
00:03:26,363 --> 00:03:29,647
Remember the galaxy that looked bigger when it was farther?

59
00:03:29,647 --> 00:03:30,259
Here's why.

60
00:03:30,959 --> 00:03:35,667
The light from MoM z fourteen has traveled thirteen and a half billion years.

61
00:03:35,667 --> 00:03:40,681
But when it left, that galaxy wasn't thirteen and a half billion light years away.

62
00:03:40,681 --> 00:03:46,551
Using the standard model of the universe, we calculated it was only about two point two billion.

63
00:03:46,551 --> 00:03:50,586
The universe was smaller then, and everything was closer together.

64
00:03:51,286 --> 00:03:54,701
So we see it at the size it had, at that closer distance.

65
00:03:54,701 --> 00:04:00,393
Its image is like an old photo, taken up close, that took billions of years to be mailed to us.

66
00:04:00,393 --> 00:04:03,508
That's why it looks larger than galaxies in between.

67
00:04:03,508 --> 00:04:07,762
The astronomer Fred Hoyle pointed this out back in nineteen fifty nine.

68
00:04:07,762 --> 00:04:11,297
It falls straight out of the math of an expanding universe.

69
00:04:11,997 --> 00:04:15,825
Now trace every light ray we receive, back to where it started.

70
00:04:15,825 --> 00:04:17,648
They make this teardrop shape.

71
00:04:17,648 --> 00:04:20,747
And the widest point of the teardrop is a surprise.

72
00:04:20,747 --> 00:04:26,946
No light that reaches us today left from farther than about five point eight billion light years away.

73
00:04:26,946 --> 00:04:32,050
The deepest pictures ever taken show things that were, at the time, relatively near.

74
00:04:32,750 --> 00:04:34,789
Wall number five is the real wall.

75
00:04:34,789 --> 00:04:38,446
Go back far enough, and the universe stops being transparent.

76
00:04:39,146 --> 00:04:44,528
For its first three hundred eighty thousand years, the universe was a hot glowing plasma.

77
00:04:44,528 --> 00:04:46,705
Electrons weren't attached to atoms.

78
00:04:46,705 --> 00:04:50,575
They floated free, and light couldn't travel in a straight line.

79
00:04:50,575 --> 00:04:54,566
A photon would hit an electron, bounce, hit another, bounce again.

80
00:04:54,566 --> 00:04:56,319
Like headlights in thick fog.

81
00:04:57,019 --> 00:05:03,271
Then it cooled to about three thousand degrees, roughly as hot as the filament of an old light bulb.

82
00:05:03,271 --> 00:05:05,584
Electrons finally settled into atoms.

83
00:05:05,584 --> 00:05:11,398
The fog cleared, and the light that was there at that moment has been flying free ever since.

84
00:05:12,098 --> 00:05:15,136
That light is still arriving, from every direction.

85
00:05:15,136 --> 00:05:21,449
Stretched about eleven hundred times, it's now microwaves, at less than three degrees above absolute zero.

86
00:05:21,449 --> 00:05:23,355
The cosmic microwave background.

87
00:05:23,355 --> 00:05:26,094
It's the farthest thing we can see with light.

88
00:05:26,094 --> 00:05:31,395
The glowing gas that sent it was only about forty two million light years away back then.

89
00:05:31,395 --> 00:05:36,695
Today, by our calculation, that same matter is about forty five billion light years away.

90
00:05:37,395 --> 00:05:40,331
After the fog cleared, the universe went dark.

91
00:05:40,331 --> 00:05:42,884
No stars yet, just cooling hydrogen gas.

92
00:05:42,884 --> 00:05:45,501
Astronomers call it the cosmic dark ages.

93
00:05:45,501 --> 00:05:52,011
Webb is now catching the very end of it, galaxies only a few hundred million years after the Big Bang.

94
00:05:52,011 --> 00:06:00,371
Radio telescopes are trying to hear the hydrogen itself, a faint signal born at twenty one centimeters, stretched by now to meters.

95
00:06:01,071 --> 00:06:04,822
Behind that glow is a wall no telescope will ever see through.

96
00:06:04,822 --> 00:06:07,604
Not because it's far, but because it's opaque.

97
00:06:07,604 --> 00:06:10,870
To look past it, we'd need something other than light.

98
00:06:10,870 --> 00:06:15,769
Ghostly particles called neutrinos, released about one second after the Big Bang.

99
00:06:15,769 --> 00:06:18,672
Or ripples in space itself, gravitational waves.

100
00:06:18,672 --> 00:06:20,184
Both should be out there.

101
00:06:20,184 --> 00:06:22,725
Neither has been caught from that era yet.

102
00:06:23,425 --> 00:06:29,917
So every direction we look, the view ends at that glowing shell, about forty six billion light years away.

103
00:06:29,917 --> 00:06:32,612
Which means we're at the center of our view.

104
00:06:32,612 --> 00:06:33,776
But so is everyone.

105
00:06:33,776 --> 00:06:41,003
An alien astronomer in MoM z fourteen would see their own bubble, centered on them, reaching places we will never see.

106
00:06:41,703 --> 00:06:43,538
And it shrinks in a sense, too.

107
00:06:43,538 --> 00:06:54,311
Because the expansion is speeding up, light that a galaxy sends today will only ever reach us if that galaxy is less than about sixteen and a half billion light years away right now.

108
00:06:54,311 --> 00:06:57,981
Farther than that, and its present is cut off from us forever.

109
00:06:58,681 --> 00:07:00,511
And what's beyond the horizon?

110
00:07:00,511 --> 00:07:02,097
Probably more of the same.

111
00:07:02,097 --> 00:07:09,477
The ancient glow looks almost identical in every direction, the same temperature to about one part in a hundred thousand.

112
00:07:09,477 --> 00:07:15,577
Nothing in it hints that our patch is special, or that the galaxies stop just past where we can see.

113
00:07:16,277 --> 00:07:18,499
So is there an edge out there at all?

114
00:07:18,499 --> 00:07:23,003
The Planck satellite measured the geometry of space from that ancient glow.

115
00:07:23,003 --> 00:07:26,306
It came out flat, to within about a fifth of a percent.

116
00:07:26,306 --> 00:07:28,348
A flat universe can go on forever.

117
00:07:28,348 --> 00:07:32,131
Or be curved so gently, on a scale so huge, that we can't tell.

118
00:07:32,131 --> 00:07:37,055
Either way, there is no sign of a wall, or a border, or a place where space stops.

119
00:07:37,755 --> 00:07:38,795
That's the twist.

120
00:07:38,795 --> 00:07:43,628
We can't see the edge of the universe, because there's probably no edge to see.

121
00:07:43,628 --> 00:07:46,197
What we have instead is a horizon in time.

122
00:07:46,197 --> 00:07:48,827
Five walls, and beyond them, more universe.

123
00:07:49,527 --> 00:07:53,600
Next time, we come back to something closer, but way bigger than it looks.

124
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:58,994
We'll put the biggest star ever measured in place of our Sun, and see what happens to the planets.

125
00:07:58,994 --> 00:08:01,580
Subscribe to Cosmic Scale so you don't miss it.

126
00:08:01,580 --> 00:08:06,423
And tell me in the comments, if you could see past the fog, what would you hope to find?
